  "notes": "Deep-bass-baritone neotraditional country with a gospel undertow. The register logic is the same documented problem as the section's other low voices and is authored the same way: keys \"set low for a bass-baritone,\" a kick \"short and dry to stay clear of the vocal fundamental,\" and a bass \"high-passed relative to the genre norm and playing simple roots — same register-collision solution as the other bass-baritone entries,\" so the bass floor is raised while its ceiling stays under the chord voicing. Drums are the stated default: \"cross-stick verses are the default, with a two-beat on the uptempo cuts.\" Harmony carries the gospel claim literally — \"I–IV–V with V7; gospel cuts add I–I7–IV–iv and a plagal amen cadence\" — so the IV–iv borrow and the IV–I plagal cadence are both families, and the dominant colour lives in `extensions` because the numeral parser reads degrees, not qualities. Melody is \"very narrow range, long notes, low tessitura, minimal ornament; the vocal timbre carries the record rather than the line.\" ⚠ Boundary flag from the research: the very fast catalogue (readings at 172–174, folding to a ~86 spine) is train-beat territory and that groove belongs to `country-train`. ⚠ Confidence is medium, matching the research; the per-track readings are [LOW-CONF]. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are the velocity tiers, the perc lane set and the counter ratio.",
